# DTF tier manifest: ondemand-index-formats  (#2589)
# Profile-set: filesystem / single. The advertised download URL is computed at
# metadata-serve time from the artifact's stored filename; this is
# storage-agnostic and the download route resolves by filename suffix, so this
# runs on the default filesystem overlay.
#
# Follow-up to #2587 (rpm primary.xml) and #2754 (cran PACKAGES / rubygems
# specs.4.8.gz). Those closed the REPO-WIDE indices. This tier covers the
# name-addressed metadata endpoints of the OTHER suffix-resolved formats whose
# on-demand response advertises a download URL reconstructed from
# `{name}-{version}` instead of the stored object's real filename:
#   * Ansible Galaxy v3 collection_info  -> `.download_url`
#   * Puppet Forge v3 module_info        -> `.current_release.file_uri`
#
# The bug: a collection/module pushed through the generic chunked-upload flow is
# stored at its bare filename with the WHOLE basename as `name` and a
# `sha256-<prefix>` fallback `version`. The metadata endpoint reconstructs
# `{namespace}-{name}-{version}.tar.gz` / `{owner}-{name}-{version}.tar.gz`,
# advertising a path the suffix-resolving download route can never serve -> 404
# even though the artifact is present and discoverable by name. The fix
# (proxy_helpers::served_download_filename) derives the advertised filename from
# the stored object path.
#
# Oracle (oracle.sh) is the discriminating gate. For BOTH formats it does a real
# generic (bare-path) chunked upload, reads the SERVED metadata JSON, extracts
# the advertised download URL, and GETs it:
#   Fixed:     advertised URL is the stored basename -> 200.
#   Pre-#2589: advertised URL is `{name}-{version}` (name=basename,
#              version=sha256 fallback) -> 404.
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: the oracle drives admin login + two generic chunked
# uploads (session + chunk + complete) + metadata/download GETs back-to-back.
PROFILES="storage.filesystem"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
